<%= UiComponents::Lookbook::TableOfContents.call(page) %>

A compact, interactive inline chart for showing a trend at a glance.
[Appica docs](https://appica.dev/ui/components/react/sparkline)

<%= embed AppicaUI::SparklinePreview, :default %>

## Usage

```ruby
Sparkline(data: [8, 14, 10, 18, 15, 22, 19, 27]) do
  SparklineChart(variant: :area, aria_label: "Signups over 8 weeks")
end
```

Sparkline is a compound component. The root owns the series, labels, formatting, and active point;
`SparklineChart` draws it; `SparklineValue` and `SparklineLabel` show the active reading (or the last
reading while idle).

Line and area charts are server-rendered SVG. Columns are ordinary HTML elements. JavaScript only
adds pointer/touch state, so the chart is complete before the controller connects.

## Examples

### Line

The default is a crisp stroke. Its hover indicator snaps to the nearest point.

<%= embed AppicaUI::SparklinePreview, :default %>

### Area

`variant: :area` fills between the curve and `baseline:` (zero by default). Values below the
baseline fill below it, so a signed series reads as magnitude rather than only direction.

<%= embed AppicaUI::SparklinePreview, :area %>

### Filled line

A line is stroke-only by default. Add `fill: true` for a gradient that fades to the bottom edge.
This works well for KPIs far from zero; use `variant: :area` when the baseline itself has meaning.

<%= embed AppicaUI::SparklinePreview, :filled_line %>

### Columns

`variant: :column` renders one bar per reading. Bars grow above or below `baseline:` and the active
bucket is highlighted on hover.

<%= embed AppicaUI::SparklinePreview, :columns %>

### Line smoothing

`curve:` ranges from `0` (straight segments) to `1` (fully rounded peaks).

<%= embed AppicaUI::SparklinePreview, :smoothing %>

### Line weight

`stroke_width:` is measured in pixels and stays the same at any chart width.

<%= embed AppicaUI::SparklinePreview, :line_weight %>

### Tooltip

Set `tooltip: true` to float the current value. Root `labels:` appear beside it.

<%= embed AppicaUI::SparklinePreview, :tooltip %>

### Value headline

Put `SparklineValue` and `SparklineLabel` anywhere inside the root. They show the final point while
idle and follow the nearest point while the chart is active.

<%= embed AppicaUI::SparklinePreview, :value_headline %>

```ruby
Sparkline(data: totals, labels: dates, class: "w-72") do
  SparklineLabel()
  SparklineValue(class: "text-2xl")
  SparklineChart(fill: true, height: 56, tooltip: true,
                 aria_label: "Active users this week")
end
```

### Reacting to the active point

The root dispatches `appica-ui--sparkline:active-change` with `{ point: { index, value, label } }`, or
`{ point: null }` when the pointer leaves or a touch ends. Connect it to another Stimulus controller
when a reading needs to appear outside the sparkline.

```ruby
Sparkline(data: totals, labels: dates,
          data: { action: "appica-ui--sparkline:active-change->dashboard#showReading" }) do
  SparklineChart(aria_label: "Weekly requests")
end
```

### Custom tooltip and text

A render block receives each point. Sparkline pre-renders those variants and the controller swaps
the active one, so custom content still works without client-side templates.

```ruby
Sparkline(data: totals, labels: dates) do
  SparklineValue { |formatted, _value| "#{formatted} users" }
  SparklineLabel { |label, point| "#{label} · day #{point[:index] + 1}" }
  SparklineChart do |point|
    strong { "#{point[:label]}: #{point[:value]}" }
  end
end
```

### Custom colors

`color:` accepts a theme token or any CSS color and drives the line, fill, indicator, columns, and
tooltip swatch.

<%= embed AppicaUI::SparklinePreview, :colors %>

### RTL

The visualization and pointer mapping mirror automatically under `dir="rtl"`.

<%= embed AppicaUI::SparklinePreview, :rtl %>

## API reference

### Sparkline

The root `<div>`. Every other HTML attribute is forwarded.

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
|------|------|---------|-------------|
| `data:` | `Array<Numeric>` | required | Series to plot. |
| `labels:` | `Array<String>` | - | Per-point labels for label and tooltip parts. |
| `color:` | CSS color | `var(--primary)` | Shared accent exposed as `--sparkline-color`. |
| `format:` | `Hash` | - | Number options: `style: :currency/:percent`, `currency:`, `maximum_fraction_digits:`. |
| `locale:` | locale | - | Rails/I18n locale for initial server formatting. |
| `class:` | `String` | - | Extra classes, merged via tailwind_merge. |

### SparklineChart

The visualization wrapper (`<div role="img">`).

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
|------|------|---------|-------------|
| `variant:` | `:line`, `:area`, `:column` | `:line` | Visualization style. |
| `curve:` | `0..1` | `0.5` | Line smoothing. |
| `fill:` | `Boolean` | `false` | Gradient fill for a line. Area is always filled. |
| `baseline:` | `Numeric` | `0` | Pivot for area and columns. |
| `height:` | `Numeric` | `48` | Chart height in pixels. |
| `stroke_width:` | `Numeric` | `2` | Line/area stroke width in pixels. |
| `indicator:` | `Boolean` | `true` | Show dot/guide or active-column highlight. |
| `tooltip:` | `Boolean` | `false` | Show a tooltip at the active point. |
| `aria_label:` | `String` | `"{variant} chart"` | Accessible graphic name; prefer a descriptive summary. |
| block | `(point) -> content` | - | Custom tooltip; implies tooltip. |

### SparklineValue

Renders the active-or-last formatted value in a `<span>`. A block receives `formatted, value` and
may render custom content.

### SparklineLabel

Renders the active-or-last label in a `<span>`, or nothing when the root has no labels. A block
receives `label, point`.

## Accessibility

- `SparklineChart` has `role="img"`. Give it a descriptive `aria_label:` that summarizes the data,
  not merely "chart".
- Tooltip and indicator overlays are `aria-hidden`; important values should also appear as text via
  `SparklineValue`, `SparklineLabel`, or an active-change listener.
- Pointer interaction is supplemental. The full trend remains visible without interaction or
  JavaScript.
- Motion classes are guarded by `motion-safe`, so transitions stop under reduced motion.
